#ifndef {{unit}}_H
#define {{unit}}_H
#include "stm32_hal.h"
#ifdef __cplusplus
extern "C" {
#endif
/* One single-ended analog input of {{converter_name}} on pad {{pad_name}}, read on
 * request. Generated init runs once after mx_system_init; failure is
 * fail-stop. The first unit on a converter brings it up: kernel clock HCLK
 * divided by 8 (18 MHz under the pinned 144 MHz profile), set before the
 * converter's bus clock is enabled and read back, then 12-bit resolution,
 * software trigger, one conversion per request, activation and the
 * converter's own offset calibration. Every unit on the same converter shares
 * that one handle; the pad is switched to analog mode by its own unit.
 * The units own the converter: the application must not initialize or
 * configure {{converter_name}} itself, and must not combine these units with an
 * analog input allocation on the same converter.
 */
hal_status_t {{unit}}_init(void);
/* Converts this input once and waits for the result. *counts is 0..4095,
 * right aligned: the pad voltage as a fraction of the reference voltage
 * VREF+, in steps of 1/4096. It is not a voltage: the reference is not
 * measured, so volts follow only from a reference the application knows.
 * Sampling uses the longest setting the SDK offers.
 * Time: every wait is the SDK's own. Each one compares the HAL ticks that
 * have passed with a limit and gives up when they exceed it: a wait ends up
 * to one tick after its limit, and it does not end while the tick stands
 * still, so the HAL tick must be advancing (interrupts enabled). These are
 * counts of ticks, not time for the caller: a thread that is delayed takes
 * longer by its delay.
 * When the sequencer already holds this input the read is one conversion,
 * for which the unit gives the SDK a limit of 2 ms. If the conversion does
 * not finish, the unit stops it before it returns HAL_TIMEOUT, and the SDK
 * waits there with a limit of 2 ms again: up to about 6 ms of ticks in
 * that case.
 * When the sequencer holds another input the converter is first stopped,
 * switched, started and calibrated again. The SDK's limits there are 2 ms
 * to disable the converter, 2 ms to enable it and 228 ms for the
 * calibration, so such a read returns after at most about a quarter of a
 * second of ticks.
 * Before a conversion the unit checks that the converter is enabled and
 * idle and that the sequencer holds this input. A converter found stopped
 * or converting behind the unit is HAL_ERROR, for this and every later read
 * on the converter. One thread only, never from an interrupt, and never
 * while another unit of the same converter is reading. HAL_BUSY,
 * HAL_TIMEOUT or HAL_ERROR leave *counts untouched.
 */
hal_status_t {{unit}}_read(uint32_t *counts);
#ifdef __cplusplus
}
#endif
#endif
